# Skills that this repository ships but did NOT author.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE MEANS
#
# Two things, and the second is the one that was missing:
#
# 1. sync_skills.sh names anything installed-but-not-produced-here under
#    "Installed but not produced here". A name listed here says that is
#    expected, and stops it being reported every run.
#
# 2. It is the repository's statement of provenance. An empty file is a claim
#    that everything under skills/ originates here -- not a default, and not
#    the absence of a claim. This repository is public and MIT-licensed, so
#    that claim is made to everyone who installs it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# UNRESOLVED (DG-263) -- these four are third-party and their licence is unknown
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Not listed as names below, because listing a name here means "expected, stop
# reporting it", and this is the opposite of settled. Written out instead, so
# nobody reads a silent file as an answer.
#
#   skills/workflow/debug-mantra/SKILL.md
#   skills/workflow/post-mortem/SKILL.md
#   skills/workflow/scrutinize/SKILL.md
#   skills/leadership/management-talk/SKILL.md
#
# All four are byte-identical to a third-party skill pack on this machine at
# ~/Documents/9arm-skills-main, and that pack's .claude-plugin/plugin.json
# lists exactly these four as its entire contents:
#
#   {"name": "9arm-skills", "skills": ["./skills/engineering/debug-mantra",
#    "./skills/engineering/post-mortem", "./skills/engineering/scrutinize",
#    "./skills/productivity/management-talk"]}
#
# So this is not four coincidental name collisions. It is one plugin, vendored.
#
# The pack carries NO LICENCE FILE. It is not a git checkout either -- the
# `-main` suffix is the shape of a GitHub zip download -- so there is no remote
# to read terms from, and nothing in the four files carries an attribution line.
#
# The four installed under ~/.claude/skills/ are symlinks pointing INTO that
# pack rather than at this repository, so what a session actually loads has
# never been this repo's copy.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS FILE PREVIOUSLY SAID THE OPPOSITE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# It said these four "were listed here as third-party with no source. Their
# source was the sibling repo all along... They are first-party now."
#
# Half of that is right and the conclusion is backwards. The sibling repo did
# have copies -- because the sibling repo had vendored them too. Finding a
# second copy of somebody else's work does not make it yours. The ORIGINAL
# entry, declaring them third-party, was correct; the merge resolved it in the
# wrong direction and this file has claimed authorship of them since.
#
# This is the same failure the merge was meant to end -- a surface asserting
# something nobody had checked -- with the sign flipped.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT HAS TO HAPPEN, AND BY WHOM
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Licensing someone else's work is not an agent's call, and it cannot be
# settled from what is on this disk. For the Boss:
#
#   a. Find the upstream and its terms. If they permit redistribution, add the
#      four as names below with the source and the licence, and add the
#      attribution the licence requires.
#   b. If they do not, or the upstream cannot be identified, move the four to
#      _not_used/ with a note. Four skills is a smaller loss than shipping
#      unlicensed work under MIT from a public repository.
#   c. If they were authored by someone reachable, ask.
#
# Until then they stay where they are, tracked and shipping, and this file says
# so out loud rather than implying otherwise.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Add a name below only when a skill is genuinely authored elsewhere AND its
# terms are known. One name per line.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
